#, no-wrap
msgid "Repository, command and file interfaces"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:361
msgid ""
"This documentation discusses repository and command interfaces which users "
"are expected to interact with directly. See `--user-formats` in "
"linkgit:git-help[1] for more details on the criteria."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:365
#, no-wrap
msgid "File formats, protocols and other developer interfaces"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:370
msgid ""
"This documentation discusses file formats, over-the-wire protocols and other "
"git developer interfaces. See `--developer-interfaces` in "
"linkgit:git-help[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:374
#, no-wrap
msgid "Configuration Mechanism"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:379
msgid ""
"Git uses a simple text format to store customizations that are per "
"repository and are per user.  Such a configuration file may look like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:384
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#\n"
"# A '#' or ';' character indicates a comment.\n"
"#\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:389
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"; core variables\n"
"[core]\n"
"\t; Don't trust file modes\n"
"\tfilemode = false\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:394
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"; user identity\n"
"[user]\n"
"\tname = \"Junio C Hamano\"\n"
"\temail = \"gitster@pobox.com\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:400
msgid ""
"Various commands read from the configuration file and adjust their operation "
"accordingly.  See linkgit:git-config[1] for a list and more details about "
"the configuration mechanism."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:403
#, no-wrap
msgid "Identifier Terminology"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:404
#, no-wrap
msgid "<object>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:406
msgid "Indicates the object name for any type of object."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:407
#, no-wrap
msgid "<blob>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:409
msgid "Indicates a blob object name."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:410
#, no-wrap
msgid "<tree>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:412
msgid "Indicates a tree object name."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:413
#, no-wrap
msgid "<commit>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:415
msgid "Indicates a commit object name."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:416
#, no-wrap
msgid "<tree-ish>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:421
msgid ""
"Indicates a tree, commit or tag object name.  A command that takes a "
"<tree-ish> argument ultimately wants to operate on a <tree> object but "
"automatically dereferences <commit> and <tag> objects that point at a "
"<tree>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:422
#, no-wrap
msgid "<commit-ish>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:427
msgid ""
"Indicates a commit or tag object name.  A command that takes a <commit-ish> "
"argument ultimately wants to operate on a <commit> object but automatically "
"dereferences <tag> objects that point at a <commit>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:428
#, no-wrap
msgid "<type>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:431
msgid ""
"Indicates that an object type is required.  Currently one of: `blob`, "
"`tree`, `commit`, or `tag`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:432
#, no-wrap
msgid "<file>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:435
msgid ""
"Indicates a filename - almost always relative to the root of the tree "
"structure `GIT_INDEX_FILE` describes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:437
#, no-wrap
msgid "Symbolic Identifiers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:440
msgid ""
"Any Git command accepting any <object> can also use the following symbolic "
"notation:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:441
#, no-wrap
msgid "HEAD"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:443
msgid "indicates the head of the current branch."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:444
#, no-wrap
msgid "<tag>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:447
msgid "a valid tag 'name' (i.e. a `refs/tags/<tag>` reference)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:448
#, no-wrap
msgid "<head>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:451
msgid "a valid head 'name' (i.e. a `refs/heads/<head>` reference)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:454
msgid ""
"For a more complete list of ways to spell object names, see \"SPECIFYING "
"REVISIONS\" section in linkgit:gitrevisions[7]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:457
#, no-wrap
msgid "File/Directory Structure"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:460
msgid "Please see the linkgit:gitrepository-layout[5] document."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:462
msgid "Read linkgit:githooks[5] for more details about each hook."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:465
msgid ""
"Higher level SCMs may provide and manage additional information in the "
"`$GIT_DIR`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:468
#, no-wrap
msgid "Terminology"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:470
msgid "Please see linkgit:gitglossary[7]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:473
#, no-wrap
msgid "Environment Variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:479
msgid ""
"Various Git commands pay attention to environment variables and change their "
"behavior.  The environment variables marked as \"Boolean\" take their values "
"the same way as Boolean valued configuration variables, i.e., \"true\", "
"\"yes\", \"on\" and positive numbers are taken as \"yes\", while \"false\", "
"\"no\", \"off\", and \"0\" are taken as \"no\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:481
msgid "Here are the variables:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ~
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:483
#, no-wrap
msgid "System"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:484
#, no-wrap
msgid "`HOME`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:489
msgid ""
"Specifies the path to the user's home directory. On Windows, if unset, Git "
"will set a process environment variable equal to: `$HOMEDRIVE$HOMEPATH` if "
"both `$HOMEDRIVE` and `$HOMEPATH` exist; otherwise `$USERPROFILE` if "
"`$USERPROFILE` exists."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ~
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:491
#, no-wrap
msgid "The Git Repository"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:495
msgid ""
"These environment variables apply to 'all' core Git commands. Nb: it is "
"worth noting that they may be used/overridden by SCMS sitting above Git so "
"take care if using a foreign front-end."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:496
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_INDEX_FILE`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:500
msgid ""
"This environment variable specifies an alternate index file. If not "
"specified, the default of `$GIT_DIR/index` is used."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:501
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_INDEX_VERSION`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:506
msgid ""
"This environment variable specifies what index version is used when writing "
"the index file out.  It won't affect existing index files.  By default index "
"file version 2 or 3 is used. See linkgit:git-update-index[1] for more "
"information."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:507
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:512
msgid ""
"If the object storage directory is specified via this environment variable "
"then the sha1 directories are created underneath - otherwise the default "
"`$GIT_DIR/objects` directory is used."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:513
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_ALTERNATE_OBJECT_DIRECTORIES`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:519
msgid ""
"Due to the immutable nature of Git objects, old objects can be archived into "
"shared, read-only directories. This variable specifies a \":\" separated (on "
"Windows \";\" separated) list of Git object directories which can be used to "
"search for Git objects. New objects will not be written to these "
"directories."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:525
msgid ""
"Entries that begin with `\"` (double-quote) will be interpreted as C-style "
"quoted paths, removing leading and trailing double-quotes and respecting "
"backslash escapes. E.g., the value "
"`\"path-with-\\\"-and-:-in-it\":vanilla-path` has two paths: "
"`path-with-\"-and-:-in-it` and `vanilla-path`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:526
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_DIR`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:531
msgid ""
"If the `GIT_DIR` environment variable is set then it specifies a path to use "
"instead of the default `.git` for the base of the repository.  The "
"`--git-dir` command-line option also sets this value."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:532
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_WORK_TREE`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:536
msgid ""
"Set the path to the root of the working tree.  This can also be controlled "
"by the `--work-tree` command-line option and the core.worktree configuration "
"variable."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:537
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_NAMESPACE`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:540
msgid ""
"Set the Git namespace; see linkgit:gitnamespaces[7] for details.  The "
"`--namespace` command-line option also sets this value."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:541
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:555
msgid ""
"This should be a colon-separated list of absolute paths.  If set, it is a "
"list of directories that Git should not chdir up into while looking for a "
"repository directory (useful for excluding slow-loading network "
"directories).  It will not exclude the current working directory or a "
"GIT_DIR set on the command line or in the environment.  Normally, Git has to "
"read the entries in this list and resolve any symlink that might be present "
"in order to compare them with the current directory.  However, if even this "
"access is slow, you can add an empty entry to the list to tell Git that the "
"subsequent entries are not symlinks and needn't be resolved; e.g., "
"`GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES=/maybe/symlink::/very/slow/non/symlink`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:556
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_DISCOVERY_ACROSS_FILESYSTEM`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:565
msgid ""
"When run in a directory that does not have \".git\" repository directory, "
"Git tries to find such a directory in the parent directories to find the top "
"of the working tree, but by default it does not cross filesystem "
"boundaries.  This Boolean environment variable can be set to true to tell "
"Git not to stop at filesystem boundaries.  Like `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES`, "
"this will not affect an explicit repository directory set via `GIT_DIR` or "
"on the command line."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:566
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_COMMON_DIR`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:574
msgid ""
"If this variable is set to a path, non-worktree files that are normally in "
"$GIT_DIR will be taken from this path instead. Worktree-specific files such "
"as HEAD or index are taken from $GIT_DIR. See "
"linkgit:gitrepository-layout[5] and linkgit:git-worktree[1] for "
"details. This variable has lower precedence than other path variables such "
"as GIT_INDEX_FILE, GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY..."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:575
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_DEFAULT_HASH`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:581
msgid ""
"If this variable is set, the default hash algorithm for new repositories "
"will be set to this value. This value is ignored when cloning and the "
"setting of the remote repository is always used. The default is \"sha1\".  "
"See `--object-format` in linkgit:git-init[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:582
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_DEFAULT_REF_FORMAT`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:586
msgid ""
"If this variable is set, the default reference backend format for new "
"repositories will be set to this value. The default is \"files\".  See "
"`--ref-format` in linkgit:git-init[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ~
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:588
#, no-wrap
msgid "Git Commits"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:589
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_AUTHOR_NAME`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:593
msgid ""
"The human-readable name used in the author identity when creating commit or "
"tag objects, or when writing reflogs. Overrides the `user.name` and "
"`author.name` configuration settings."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:594
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:598
msgid ""
"The email address used in the author identity when creating commit or tag "
"objects, or when writing reflogs. Overrides the `user.email` and "
"`author.email` configuration settings."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:599
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_AUTHOR_DATE`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:602
msgid ""
"The date used for the author identity when creating commit or tag objects, "
"or when writing reflogs. See linkgit:git-commit[1] for valid formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:603
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_COMMITTER_NAME`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:607
msgid ""
"The human-readable name used in the committer identity when creating commit "
"or tag objects, or when writing reflogs. Overrides the `user.name` and "
"`committer.name` configuration settings."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:608
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:612
msgid ""
"The email address used in the author identity when creating commit or tag "
"objects, or when writing reflogs. Overrides the `user.email` and "
"`committer.email` configuration settings."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:613
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_COMMITTER_DATE`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:616
msgid ""
"The date used for the committer identity when creating commit or tag "
"objects, or when writing reflogs. See linkgit:git-commit[1] for valid "
"formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:617
#, no-wrap
msgid "`EMAIL`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:620
msgid ""
"The email address used in the author and committer identities if no other "
"relevant environment variable or configuration setting has been set."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ~
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:622
#, no-wrap
msgid "Git Diffs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:623
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_DIFF_OPTS`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:628
msgid ""
"Only valid setting is \"--unified=??\" or \"-u??\" to set the number of "
"context lines shown when a unified diff is created.  This takes precedence "
"over any \"-U\" or \"--unified\" option value passed on the Git diff command "
"line."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:629
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_EXTERNAL_DIFF`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:635
msgid ""
"When the environment variable `GIT_EXTERNAL_DIFF` is set, the program named "
"by it is called to generate diffs, and Git does not use its builtin diff "
"machinery.  For a path that is added, removed, or modified, "
"`GIT_EXTERNAL_DIFF` is called with 7 parameters:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:637
msgid "path old-file old-hex old-mode new-file new-hex new-mode"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:639
msgid "where:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:641
#, no-wrap
msgid "<old|new>-file"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:643
msgid "are files GIT_EXTERNAL_DIFF can use to read the contents of <old|new>,"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:643
#, no-wrap
msgid "<old|new>-hex"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:644
msgid "are the 40-hexdigit SHA-1 hashes,"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:644
#, no-wrap
msgid "<old|new>-mode"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:645
msgid "are the octal representation of the file modes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:652
msgid ""
"The file parameters can point at the user's working file (e.g. `new-file` in "
"\"git-diff-files\"), `/dev/null` (e.g. `old-file` when a new file is added), "
"or a temporary file (e.g. `old-file` in the index).  `GIT_EXTERNAL_DIFF` "
"should not worry about unlinking the temporary file -- it is removed when "
"`GIT_EXTERNAL_DIFF` exits."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:655
msgid ""
"For a path that is unmerged, `GIT_EXTERNAL_DIFF` is called with 1 parameter, "
"<path>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:658
msgid ""
"For each path `GIT_EXTERNAL_DIFF` is called, two environment variables, "
"`GIT_DIFF_PATH_COUNTER` and `GIT_DIFF_PATH_TOTAL` are set."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:659
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_EXTERNAL_DIFF_TRUST_EXIT_CODE`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:667
msgid ""
"If this Boolean environment variable is set to true then the "
"`GIT_EXTERNAL_DIFF` command is expected to return exit code 0 if it "
"considers the input files to be equal or 1 if it considers them to be "
"different, like `diff(1)`.  If it is set to false, which is the default, "
"then the command is expected to return exit code 0 regardless of equality.  "
"Any other exit code causes Git to report a fatal error."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:669
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_DIFF_PATH_COUNTER`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:671
msgid "A 1-based counter incremented by one for every path."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:672
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_DIFF_PATH_TOTAL`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:674
msgid "The total number of paths."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ~
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:676
#, no-wrap
msgid "other"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:677
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_MERGE_VERBOSITY`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:681
msgid ""
"A number controlling the amount of output shown by the recursive merge "
"strategy.  Overrides merge.verbosity.  See linkgit:git-merge[1]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:682
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_PAGER`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:687
msgid ""
"This environment variable overrides `$PAGER`. If it is set to an empty "
"string or to the value \"cat\", Git will not launch a pager.  See also the "
"`core.pager` option in linkgit:git-config[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:688
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_PROGRESS_DELAY`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:691
msgid ""
"A number controlling how many seconds to delay before showing optional "
"progress indicators. Defaults to 1."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:692
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_EDITOR`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:697
msgid ""
"This environment variable overrides `$EDITOR` and `$VISUAL`.  It is used by "
"several Git commands when, on interactive mode, an editor is to be "
"launched. See also linkgit:git-var[1] and the `core.editor` option in "
"linkgit:git-config[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:698
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_SEQUENCE_EDITOR`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:703
msgid ""
"This environment variable overrides the configured Git editor when editing "
"the todo list of an interactive rebase. See also linkgit:git-rebase[1] and "
"the `sequence.editor` option in linkgit:git-config[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:704
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_SSH`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:705
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_SSH_COMMAND`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:712
msgid ""
"If either of these environment variables is set then 'git fetch' and 'git "
"push' will use the specified command instead of 'ssh' when they need to "
"connect to a remote system.  The command-line parameters passed to the "
"configured command are determined by the ssh variant.  See `ssh.variant` "
"option in linkgit:git-config[1] for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:718
msgid ""
"`$GIT_SSH_COMMAND` takes precedence over `$GIT_SSH`, and is interpreted by "
"the shell, which allows additional arguments to be included.  `$GIT_SSH` on "
"the other hand must be just the path to a program (which can be a wrapper "
"shell script, if additional arguments are needed)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:722
msgid ""
"Usually it is easier to configure any desired options through your personal "
"`.ssh/config` file.  Please consult your ssh documentation for further "
"details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:723
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_SSH_VARIANT`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:728
msgid ""
"If this environment variable is set, it overrides Git's autodetection "
"whether `GIT_SSH`/`GIT_SSH_COMMAND`/`core.sshCommand` refer to OpenSSH, "
"plink or tortoiseplink. This variable overrides the config setting "
"`ssh.variant` that serves the same purpose."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:729
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_SSL_NO_VERIFY`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:733
msgid ""
"Setting and exporting this environment variable to any value tells Git not "
"to verify the SSL certificate when fetching or pushing over HTTPS."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:734
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_ATTR_SOURCE`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:736
msgid "Sets the treeish that gitattributes will be read from."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:737
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_ASKPASS`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:743
msgid ""
"If this environment variable is set, then Git commands which need to acquire "
"passwords or passphrases (e.g. for HTTP or IMAP authentication)  will call "
"this program with a suitable prompt as command-line argument and read the "
"password from its STDOUT. See also the `core.askPass` option in "
"linkgit:git-config[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:744
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:747
msgid ""
"If this Boolean environment variable is set to false, git will not prompt on "
"the terminal (e.g., when asking for HTTP authentication)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:748
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:749
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:757
msgid ""
"Take the configuration from the given files instead from global or "
"system-level configuration files. If `GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM` is set, the system "
"config file defined at build time (usually `/etc/gitconfig`)  will not be "
"read. Likewise, if `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL` is set, neither `$HOME/.gitconfig` "
"nor `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git/config` will be read. Can be set to `/dev/null` to "
"skip reading configuration files of the respective level."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:758
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:765
msgid ""
"Whether to skip reading settings from the system-wide "
"`$(prefix)/etc/gitconfig` file.  This Boolean environment variable can be "
"used along with `$HOME` and `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME` to create a predictable "
"environment for a picky script, or you can set it to true to temporarily "
"avoid using a buggy `/etc/gitconfig` file while waiting for someone with "
"sufficient permissions to fix it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:766
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_FLUSH`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:776
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If this Boolean environment variable is set to true, then commands such\n"
"as 'git blame' (in incremental mode), 'git rev-list', 'git log',\n"
"'git check-attr' and 'git check-ignore' will\n"
"force a flush of the output stream after each record have been\n"
"flushed. If this\n"
"variable is set to false, the output of these commands will be done\n"
"using completely buffered I/O.   If this environment variable is\n"
"not set, Git will choose buffered or record-oriented flushing\n"
"based on whether stdout appears to be redirected to a file or not.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:777
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_TRACE`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:780
msgid ""
"Enables general trace messages, e.g. alias expansion, built-in command "
"execution and external command execution."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:784 ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:864
msgid ""
"If this variable is set to \"1\", \"2\" or \"true\" (comparison is case "
"insensitive), trace messages will be printed to stderr."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:789 ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:869
msgid ""
"If the variable is set to an integer value greater than 2 and lower than 10 "
"(strictly) then Git will interpret this value as an open file descriptor and "
"will try to write the trace messages into this file descriptor."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:794
msgid ""
"Alternatively, if the variable is set to an absolute path (starting with a "
"'/' character), Git will interpret this as a file path and will try to "
"append the trace messages to it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:797 ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:886
msgid ""
"Unsetting the variable, or setting it to empty, \"0\" or \"false\" (case "
"insensitive) disables trace messages."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:798
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_TRACE_FSMONITOR`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:801
msgid ""
"Enables trace messages for the filesystem monitor extension.  See "
"`GIT_TRACE` for available trace output options."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:802
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_TRACE_PACK_ACCESS`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:808
msgid ""
"Enables trace messages for all accesses to any packs. For each access, the "
"pack file name and an offset in the pack is recorded. This may be helpful "
"for troubleshooting some pack-related performance problems.  See `GIT_TRACE` "
"for available trace output options."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:809
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_TRACE_PACKET`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:815
msgid ""
"Enables trace messages for all packets coming in or out of a given "
"program. This can help with debugging object negotiation or other protocol "
"issues. Tracing is turned off at a packet starting with \"PACK\" (but see "
"`GIT_TRACE_PACKFILE` below).  See `GIT_TRACE` for available trace output "
"options."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:816
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_TRACE_PACKFILE`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:823
msgid ""
"Enables tracing of packfiles sent or received by a given program. Unlike "
"other trace output, this trace is verbatim: no headers, and no quoting of "
"binary data. You almost certainly want to direct into a file (e.g., "
"`GIT_TRACE_PACKFILE=/tmp/my.pack`) rather than displaying it on the terminal "
"or mixing it with other trace output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:826
msgid ""
"Note that this is currently only implemented for the client side of clones "
"and fetches."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:827
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_TRACE_PERFORMANCE`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:831
msgid ""
"Enables performance related trace messages, e.g. total execution time of "
"each Git command.  See `GIT_TRACE` for available trace output options."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:832
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_TRACE_REFS`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:835
msgid ""
"Enables trace messages for operations on the ref database.  See `GIT_TRACE` "
"for available trace output options."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:836
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_TRACE_SETUP`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:840
msgid ""
"Enables trace messages printing the .git, working tree and current working "
"directory after Git has completed its setup phase.  See `GIT_TRACE` for "
"available trace output options."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:841
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_TRACE_SHALLOW`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:845
msgid ""
"Enables trace messages that can help debugging fetching / cloning of shallow "
"repositories.  See `GIT_TRACE` for available trace output options."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:846
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_TRACE_CURL`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:851
msgid ""
"Enables a curl full trace dump of all incoming and outgoing data, including "
"descriptive information, of the git transport protocol.  This is similar to "
"doing curl `--trace-ascii` on the command line.  See `GIT_TRACE` for "
"available trace output options."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:852
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_TRACE_CURL_NO_DATA`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:855
msgid ""
"When a curl trace is enabled (see `GIT_TRACE_CURL` above), do not dump data "
"(that is, only dump info lines and headers)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:856
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_TRACE2`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:860
msgid ""
"Enables more detailed trace messages from the \"trace2\" library.  Output "
"from `GIT_TRACE2` is a simple text-based format for human readability."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:878
msgid ""
"Alternatively, if the variable is set to an absolute path (starting with a "
"'/' character), Git will interpret this as a file path and will try to "
"append the trace messages to it.  If the path already exists and is a "
"directory, the trace messages will be written to files (one per process)  in "
"that directory, named according to the last component of the SID and an "
"optional counter (to avoid filename collisions)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:883
msgid ""
"In addition, if the variable is set to "
"`af_unix:[<socket-type>:]<absolute-pathname>`, Git will try to open the path "
"as a Unix Domain Socket.  The socket type can be either `stream` or `dgram`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:889
msgid "See link:technical/api-trace2.html[Trace2 documentation] for full details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:891
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_TRACE2_EVENT`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:896
msgid ""
"This setting writes a JSON-based format that is suited for machine "
"interpretation.  See `GIT_TRACE2` for available trace output options and "
"link:technical/api-trace2.html[Trace2 documentation] for full details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:897
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_TRACE2_PERF`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:903
msgid ""
"In addition to the text-based messages available in `GIT_TRACE2`, this "
"setting writes a column-based format for understanding nesting regions.  See "
"`GIT_TRACE2` for available trace output options and "
"link:technical/api-trace2.html[Trace2 documentation] for full details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:904
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_TRACE_REDACT`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:909
msgid ""
"By default, when tracing is activated, Git redacts the values of cookies, "
"the \"Authorization:\" header, the \"Proxy-Authorization:\" header and "
"packfile URIs. Set this Boolean environment variable to false to prevent "
"this redaction."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:910
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_NO_REPLACE_OBJECTS`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:913
msgid ""
"Setting and exporting this environment variable tells Git to ignore "
"replacement refs and do not replace Git objects."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:914
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:922
msgid ""
"Setting this Boolean environment variable to true will cause Git to treat "
"all pathspecs literally, rather than as glob patterns. For example, running "
"`GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=1 git log -- '*.c'` will search for commits that "
"touch the path `*.c`, not any paths that the glob `*.c` matches. You might "
"want this if you are feeding literal paths to Git (e.g., paths previously "
"given to you by `git ls-tree`, `--raw` diff output, etc)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:923
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_GLOB_PATHSPECS`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:926
msgid ""
"Setting this Boolean environment variable to true will cause Git to treat "
"all pathspecs as glob patterns (aka \"glob\" magic)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:927
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_NOGLOB_PATHSPECS`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:930
msgid ""
"Setting this Boolean environment variable to true will cause Git to treat "
"all pathspecs as literal (aka \"literal\" magic)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:931
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_ICASE_PATHSPECS`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:934
msgid ""
"Setting this Boolean environment variable to true will cause Git to treat "
"all pathspecs as case-insensitive."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:935
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:939
msgid ""
"Setting this Boolean environment variable to true tells Git not to lazily "
"fetch missing objects from the promisor remote on demand."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:940
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_REFLOG_ACTION`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:949
msgid ""
"When a ref is updated, reflog entries are created to keep track of the "
"reason why the ref was updated (which is typically the name of the "
"high-level command that updated the ref), in addition to the old and new "
"values of the ref.  A scripted Porcelain command can use set_reflog_action "
"helper function in `git-sh-setup` to set its name to this variable when it "
"is invoked as the top level command by the end user, to be recorded in the "
"body of the reflog."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:950
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_REF_PARANOIA`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:961
msgid ""
"If this Boolean environment variable is set to false, ignore broken or badly "
"named refs when iterating over lists of refs. Normally Git will try to "
"include any such refs, which may cause some operations to fail. This is "
"usually preferable, as potentially destructive operations (e.g., "
"linkgit:git-prune[1]) are better off aborting rather than ignoring broken "
"refs (and thus considering the history they point to as not worth "
"saving). The default value is `1` (i.e., be paranoid about detecting and "
"aborting all operations). You should not normally need to set this to `0`, "
"but it may be useful when trying to salvage data from a corrupted "
"repository."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:962
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_COMMIT_GRAPH_PARANOIA`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:967
msgid ""
"When loading a commit object from the commit-graph, Git performs an "
"existence check on the object in the object database. This is done to avoid "
"issues with stale commit-graphs that contain references to already-deleted "
"commits, but comes with a performance penalty."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:971
msgid ""
"The default is \"false\", which disables the aforementioned behavior.  "
"Setting this to \"true\" enables the existence check so that stale commits "
"will never be returned from the commit-graph at the cost of performance."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:972
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:978
msgid ""
"If set to a colon-separated list of protocols, behave as if `protocol.allow` "
"is set to `never`, and each of the listed protocols has "
"`protocol.<name>.allow` set to `always` (overriding any existing "
"configuration). See the description of `protocol.allow` in "
"linkgit:git-config[1] for more details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:979
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_PROTOCOL_FROM_USER`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:985
msgid ""
"Set this Boolean environment variable to false to prevent protocols used by "
"fetch/push/clone which are configured to the `user` state.  This is useful "
"to restrict recursive submodule initialization from an untrusted repository "
"or for programs which feed potentially-untrusted URLS to git commands.  See "
"linkgit:git-config[1] for more details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:986
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_PROTOCOL`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:991
msgid ""
"For internal use only.  Used in handshaking the wire protocol.  Contains a "
"colon ':' separated list of keys with optional values '<key>[=<value>]'.  "
"Presence of unknown keys and values must be ignored."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1000
msgid ""
"Note that servers may need to be configured to allow this variable to pass "
"over some transports. It will be propagated automatically when accessing "
"local repositories (i.e., `file://` or a filesystem path), as well as over "
"the `git://` protocol. For git-over-http, it should work automatically in "
"most configurations, but see the discussion in "
"linkgit:git-http-backend[1]. For git-over-ssh, the ssh server may need to be "
"configured to allow clients to pass this variable (e.g., by using `AcceptEnv "
"GIT_PROTOCOL` with OpenSSH)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1006
msgid ""
"This configuration is optional. If the variable is not propagated, then "
"clients will fall back to the original \"v0\" protocol (but may miss out on "
"some performance improvements or features). This variable currently only "
"affects clones and fetches; it is not yet used for pushes (but may be in the "
"future)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1007
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1014
msgid ""
"If this Boolean environment variable is set to false, Git will complete any "
"requested operation without performing any optional sub-operations that "
"require taking a lock.  For example, this will prevent `git status` from "
"refreshing the index as a side effect. This is useful for processes running "
"in the background which do not want to cause lock contention with other "
"operations on the repository.  Defaults to `1`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1015
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_REDIRECT_STDIN`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1016
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_REDIRECT_STDOUT`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1017
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_REDIRECT_STDERR`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1027
msgid ""
"Windows-only: allow redirecting the standard input/output/error handles to "
"paths specified by the environment variables. This is particularly useful in "
"multi-threaded applications where the canonical way to pass standard handles "
"via `CreateProcess()` is not an option because it would require the handles "
"to be marked inheritable (and consequently *every* spawned process would "
"inherit them, possibly blocking regular Git operations). The primary "
"intended use case is to use named pipes for communication "
"(e.g. `\\\\.\\pipe\\my-git-stdin-123`)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1032
msgid ""
"Two special values are supported: `off` will simply close the corresponding "
"standard handle, and if `GIT_REDIRECT_STDERR` is `2>&1`, standard error will "
"be redirected to the same handle as standard output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1033
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_PRINT_SHA1_ELLIPSIS` (deprecated)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1041
msgid ""
"If set to `yes`, print an ellipsis following an (abbreviated) SHA-1 value.  "
"This affects indications of detached HEADs (linkgit:git-checkout[1]) and the "
"raw diff output (linkgit:git-diff[1]).  Printing an ellipsis in the cases "
"mentioned is no longer considered adequate and support for it is likely to "
"be removed in the foreseeable future (along with the variable)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1042
#, no-wrap
msgid "`GIT_ADVICE`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1052
msgid ""
"If set to `0`, then disable all advice messages. These messages are intended "
"to provide hints to human users that may help them get out of problematic "
"situations or take advantage of new features. Users can disable individual "
"messages using the `advice.*` config keys. These messages may be disruptive "
"to tools that execute Git processes, so this variable is available to "
"disable the messages. (The `--no-advice` global option is also available, "
"but old Git versions may fail when this option is not understood. The "
"environment variable will be ignored by Git versions that do not understand "
"it.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1054
#, no-wrap
msgid "Discussion[[Discussion]]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1059
msgid ""
"More detail on the following is available from the "
"link:user-manual.html#git-concepts[Git concepts chapter of the user-manual] "
"and linkgit:gitcore-tutorial[7]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1066
msgid ""
"A Git project normally consists of a working directory with a \".git\" "
"subdirectory at the top level.  The .git directory contains, among other "
"things, a compressed object database representing the complete history of "
"the project, an \"index\" file which links that history to the current "
"contents of the working tree, and named pointers into that history such as "
"tags and branch heads."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1071
msgid ""
"The object database contains objects of three main types: blobs, which hold "
"file data; trees, which point to blobs and other trees to build up directory "
"hierarchies; and commits, which each reference a single tree and some number "
"of parent commits."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1076
msgid ""
"The commit, equivalent to what other systems call a \"changeset\" or "
"\"version\", represents a step in the project's history, and each parent "
"represents an immediately preceding step.  Commits with more than one parent "
"represent merges of independent lines of development."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1082
msgid ""
"All objects are named by the SHA-1 hash of their contents, normally written "
"as a string of 40 hex digits.  Such names are globally unique.  The entire "
"history leading up to a commit can be vouched for by signing just that "
"commit.  A fourth object type, the tag, is provided for this purpose."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1085
msgid ""
"When first created, objects are stored in individual files, but for "
"efficiency may later be compressed together into \"pack files\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1093
msgid ""
"Named pointers called refs mark interesting points in history.  A ref may "
"contain the SHA-1 name of an object or the name of another ref (the latter "
"is called a \"symbolic ref\").  Refs with names beginning `refs/head/` "
"contain the SHA-1 name of the most recent commit (or \"head\") of a branch "
"under development.  SHA-1 names of tags of interest are stored under "
"`refs/tags/`.  A symbolic ref named `HEAD` contains the name of the "
"currently checked-out branch."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1102
msgid ""
"The index file is initialized with a list of all paths and, for each path, a "
"blob object and a set of attributes.  The blob object represents the "
"contents of the file as of the head of the current branch.  The attributes "
"(last modified time, size, etc.) are taken from the corresponding file in "
"the working tree.  Subsequent changes to the working tree can be found by "
"comparing these attributes.  The index may be updated with new content, and "
"new commits may be created from the content stored in the index."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1106
msgid ""
"The index is also capable of storing multiple entries (called \"stages\")  "
"for a given pathname.  These stages are used to hold the various unmerged "
"version of a file when a merge is in progress."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1108
#, no-wrap
msgid "SECURITY"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1114
msgid ""
"Some configuration options and hook files may cause Git to run arbitrary "
"shell commands. Because configuration and hooks are not copied using `git "
"clone`, it is generally safe to clone remote repositories with untrusted "
"content, inspect them with `git log`, and so on."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1119
msgid ""
"However, it is not safe to run Git commands in a `.git` directory (or the "
"working tree that surrounds it) when that `.git` directory itself comes from "
"an untrusted source. The commands in its config and hooks are executed in "
"the usual way."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1127
msgid ""
"By default, Git will refuse to run when the repository is owned by someone "
"other than the user running the command. See the entry for `safe.directory` "
"in linkgit:git-config[1]. While this can help protect you in a multi-user "
"environment, note that you can also acquire untrusted repositories that are "
"owned by you (for example, if you extract a zip file or tarball from an "
"untrusted source). In such cases, you'd need to \"sanitize\" the untrusted "
"repository first."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1137
msgid ""
"If you have an untrusted `.git` directory, you should first clone it with "
"`git clone --no-local` to obtain a clean copy. Git does restrict the set of "
"options and hooks that will be run by `upload-pack`, which handles the "
"server side of a clone or fetch, but beware that the surface area for attack "
"against `upload-pack` is large, so this does carry some risk. The safest "
"thing is to serve the repository as an unprivileged user (either via "
"linkgit:git-daemon[1], ssh, or using other tools to change user ids). See "
"the discussion in the `SECURITY` section of linkgit:git-upload-pack[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1139
#, no-wrap
msgid "FURTHER DOCUMENTATION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1144
msgid ""
"See the references in the \"description\" section to get started using Git.  "
"The following is probably more detail than necessary for a first-time user."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.adoc:1148
msgid ""
"The link:user-manual.html#git-concepts[Git concepts chapter of the "
"user-manual] and linkgit:gitcore-tutorial[7] both provide introductions to "
"the underlying Git architecture."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
